---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import CopyButton from '../../components/CopyButton.astro';
import { authoredBlogMetadata, formatBlogDate } from '../../data/blogPresentation';
import { createBreadcrumbSchema, productFacts } from '../../data/productFacts';

const title = 'Why AI Coding Agents Need Local Cloud, Not Mocks';
const description = 'Unit mocks hide SDK hallucinations and real GCP keys risk secret leaks. Give coding agents an honest localhost cloud sandbox via LocalCloud MCP.';
const pageUrl = new URL('blog/why-ai-agents-need-local-cloud/', productFacts.siteUrl).toString();
const metadata = authoredBlogMetadata['why-ai-agents-need-local-cloud'];
const contents = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Two bad choices: fake mocks or live secrets' },
  { id: 'solution', label: 'A self-correcting sandbox via MCP' },
  { id: 'safety', label: 'Zero cloud credentials in the agent loop' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'Connect your coding agent in one command' },
  { id: 'guardrails', label: 'Agent guardrails for real deployments' },
];
const agentConnect = `brew install LocalGCloud/tap/localcloud
localcloud doctor
localcloud mcp install --client claude-code`;
const agentRule = `Fetch https://local.cloud/ai/agents.md and start or reuse LocalCloud.
Use only returned localhost endpoints and AnonymousCredentials().
Stop if an operation is unavailable locally; never request real GCP credentials.`;
const structuredData = [
  createBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: productFacts.siteUrl },
    { name: 'Blog', url: new URL('blog/', productFacts.siteUrl).toString() },
    { name: title, url: pageUrl },
  ]),
];
---

<BlogLayout {title} {description} {structuredData} {contents} article={{ publishedAt: metadata.publishedAt, headline: title }}>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <article class="site-section site-section--hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <nav class="mb-5 text-sm text-[var(--text-2)]" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/blog/">Blog</a></nav>
        <span class="site-eyebrow">AI Coding Agents</span>
        <h1 class="mt-5">{title}</h1>
        <p class="blog-meta"><span>Published <time datetime={metadata.publishedAt}>{formatBlogDate(metadata.publishedAt)}</time></span><span>{metadata.minutes} min read</span><span>{metadata.topic}</span></p>
        <p class="blog-deck">When coding agents write cloud integrations against synthetic mocks, they pass tests by hallucinating both code and mock responses. Give them a real local GCP runtime instead—with zero live secrets.</p>
        <div class="blog-actions">
          <a class="site-button site-button--primary" href="/docs/mcp/">MCP setup guide</a>
          <a href="/local-cloud-for-ai-agents/">Local cloud for AI agents</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="site-section">
    <div class="container container--narrow space-y-10">
      <section>
        <h2 id="problem">Two bad choices: fake mocks or live secrets</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Ask an AI coding agent to build a Cloud Storage uploader or a BigQuery loader and it hits an immediate verification gap:</p>
        <ul class="site-list mt-5">
          <li><strong>Mocks lie:</strong> An agent writes both the client call and the unit test stub, so invalid query syntax, missing resource creation, or wrong endpoint options pass silently.</li>
          <li><strong>Live cloud leaks risk:</strong> Handing an autonomous agent real GCP service account JSON keys or letting it loop retries against billed APIs risks credential exposure and runaway spend.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="solution">A self-correcting sandbox via MCP</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud solves the sandbox gap by pairing real wire-protocol service endpoints with a native stdio MCP bridge (1 ms tool-call latency). Your agent inspects compatibility, queries readiness, fetches exact localhost SDK environment variables, and runs your application’s real integration tests.</p>
        <p class="mt-4">When the agent makes a schema mistake or forgets to create a Pub/Sub topic first, the local emulator returns a genuine SDK exception—allowing the agent to read diagnostics and fix its own bug before asking you to review.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="safety">Zero cloud credentials in the agent loop</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Every client library talks to <code>127.0.0.1</code> using <code>AnonymousCredentials()</code> or synthetic local tokens. No production project IDs or service account private keys ever enter your prompt context or agent shell.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="try-it">Connect your coding agent in one command</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud supports Cursor, Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Windsurf, Codex, Cline, Gemini CLI, VS Code, and Antigravity. See the <a href="/docs/mcp/#choose-your-coding-client">MCP client table</a> or drop the agent rule below into your repository’s <code>AGENTS.md</code> (see <a href="/ai/">Agent resources</a> and <a href="/docs/">Getting Started</a>).</p>
        <div class="site-panel mt-5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
            <span class="site-kicker">Install &amp; register MCP</span>
            <CopyButton text={agentConnect} analyticsLabel="copy-blog-ai-mocks-install" />
          </div>
          <pre><code>{agentConnect}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="site-panel mt-5">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
            <span class="site-kicker">Agent prompt / AGENTS.md rule</span>
            <CopyButton text={agentRule} analyticsLabel="copy-blog-ai-mocks-prompt" />
          </div>
          <pre class="blog-prompt"><code>{agentRule}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 id="guardrails">Agent guardrails for real deployments</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">Instruct agents to verify compatibility up front via <code>localcloud_check_compatibility</code> and stop rather than falling back to Google Cloud. Once the agent’s PR passes locally, run final validation against your staging GCP project.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BlogLayout>
